@startuml remote-worker-retire
' Revision 2026-10-12: R-OPERATOR operator custody/act gate; formats and qualification remain open.
' Revision 2026-10-12: R-WORKPLACE service controls, helper-v1 activation and execution-level stops; Draft, no renderer/qualification evidence.
title Lifecycle of a remote worker (revision 6; variant-aware cleanup and independent state axes): stop, leave and purge on the donor; suspend, retire and revoke on our side; the identity stays pinned unless revoked

actor "Operator (our side)" as OP
participant "Registry / key keeper" as T
participant "swarma-dispatcher + swarma-user-manager\n(project host; account lifecycle)" as D
participant "Service-principal bridge + swarma-rb-exec\nroot sshd + relay-only fixed shell" as BR
actor "Donor (the machine's owner)" as ADM
participant "curator remote-worker\n(donor bridge role; UID follows variant)" as C
participant "Separate donor provisioner (B only)\n(binary/schema/rules/removal pending; B disabled)" as PH

note right of OP
  R-OPERATOR: project/platform operator acts use no-harness swarma-operator.
  Phone main: hardware-backed presence-gated operator device key; displayed act signed,
  pinned-key SSH over tailnet; forced operator command, no shell; canonical binding/no reuse.
  Terminal fallback: password sudo, timestamp_timeout=0, no NOPASSWD.
  Widening authenticates + confirms exact content; refuses from an agent session.
  Narrowing authenticates only; phone revocation is one biometric confirmation.
  Reads use mandatory swarma-observer without confirmation.
  Delegated agent acts stay under their own principal/ceiling, never operator UID.
  Existing record domains/algorithms unchanged; command/record formats and key lifecycle open.
  [[../CIP-0011-credential-broker-and-agent-users.md#5-curator-broker R-OPERATOR rules and widening list]]
end note

note over OP, PH
  Independent axes: installation present/purged; donor execution active/stopped/left;
  project workplace admitted/suspended/retired; signing identity pinned/revoked.
  A/C share donor UID; B has separate UIDs. Offline purge proves no remote revocation.
  Stop and revocation marks are persisted BEFORE any process is touched, so a service restart cannot race them.
  User-certificate revocation/expiry never substitutes for closing established sessions and killing jobs.
end note

== donor: stop (pause) ==
ADM -> C: curator remote-worker stop
C -> C: persist "stopped"; end this worker's harness and descendants (never all donor-UID processes); keep the bridge service and the keys; audit line
C --> ADM: stopped (run needs valid admission/credentials/qualification)

== donor: leave (retire the workplace, keep the identity) ==
ADM -> C: curator remote-worker leave
C -> BR: retire request over the channel (if reachable)
C -> C: close the channel; keep K_sig, K_enc, K_ssh and the configuration
C --> ADM: left; "init --rejoin" with a new invitation returns the pinned identity to a newly admitted workplace unless revoked

== project: suspend / retire / revoke ==
OP -> T: suspend: grants paused
OP -> BR: suspend: close sessions; keep workplace
OP -> D: retire workplace
D -> BR: persist channel/grant revocation; stop renewal; user-certificate KRL; close sessions
D -> D: journaled exec.stop for workplace executions via receipt/effect;\nconfirm absence under published account-wide stop scope; no process-group-only proof
break process absence not established
  D -> D: retain account/egress; reconcile unknown evidence; do not delete home
end
D -> D: journal declared-key cleanup, then delete workplace home;\nexport service-generated closed-schema metadata only (no agent-controlled strings/names/links/bodies)
break key cleanup / home deletion / metadata validation failure
  D -> D: do not release remaining account/egress; journaled recovery; exact format unpublished
end
D -> D: confirmed absence + key cleanup + home deletion -> release account and egress rules
BR -> BR: next frame or envelope from this worker refused (retired); mailbox kept durable
note right of BR: the donor's connection drops; its bridge reports "retired by project" and pauses
OP -> T: revoke (only on compromise or departure): root-signed revocation of K_sig, K_enc, K_ssh
T -> BR: persist revocation; user-certificate KRL; close sessions; stop renewal;\nrevocation record: every frame and envelope from these keys refuses from now on;\na return needs a new key through a new enrolment (rotation is a root-authorized record)

== donor: purge (destroy) ==
ADM -> C: curator remote-worker purge --confirm
C -> BR: report purge; ask revocation outcome (if reachable)
alt channel reachable
  BR --> C: reported workplace and identity states (independent axes; response schema pending)
else channel unreachable
  C -> C: record "channel unreachable; the project side must revoke separately"
end
C -> C: stop owned processes; delete keys, locked config, managed home; retain donor-visible audit
alt A
  C -> C: remove owned user service/artifacts; preserve donor account and unrelated data
else C
  C -> C: remove owned sandbox + user service/artifacts; preserve donor account
else B (future path, disabled while provisioner contract pending)
  C -> PH: remove only B-allocated accounts, egress, sandbox, system service\n(exact closed operation format pending; journal/rollback required)
  PH --> C: removal result
end
C --> ADM: variant-owned artifacts removed; printed revocation result or unknown when unreachable
note over OP, PH
  Phase: Draft donor target after one-host slice 0 + project helper v1/workplace qualification; A first, B disabled, C fallback.
  Donor Curator human commands run as donor UID; bridge service owns lifecycle marks,
  keys/config/audit (A/C donor UID; B bridge UID); harness owns home/scratch.
  Separate explicit operator home retention states confidentiality is not guaranteed.
  Git/accepted outputs have a separate confidentiality policy.
  Project dispatcher service owns tracking, root user-manager owns account lifecycle;
  root sshd owns relay shell/user KRL; service executor owns claims/floors/audit;
  project bridge owns channel/mailbox state; user CA private key stays with keeper/hardware token;
  registry/keeper owns signed identity revocations. Their exact UIDs/storage formats
  unspecified in spec CIP-0009 D7/D12. Stop terminates only owned harness descendants.
  Cleanup cannot release workplace before proven process absence; forward-recovery
  states unspecified in spec CIP-0009 D7. Local purge never proves remote revocation.
  [[../CIP-0009-donor-side-deployment-and-bridge.md#recommendation CIP-0009 D7/D12]]
  [[../../protocol/remote-worker.md#tools-mailbox-and-lifecycle Four independent axes]]
end note

@enduml
